WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:03.850
(upbeat music)

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.830
- Hello, we are continuing with our survey

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.950
of the national birds of
Latin American countries.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:12.020
And we are here and
continuing with the Caribbean.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.450
That this tray here has
the national birds of most

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.470
we're missing maybe a couple
of the Caribbean islands.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.823
And I apologize for that.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.330
So start here with the northern,

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.350
with the Greater Antilles
and the Island of Cuba,

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:30.050
Cuba selected the trogon.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.110
This is a beautiful species

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:37.053
and even has the colors
matching the Cuban flag.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.900
The Cuban name is tocororo or tocororo,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.450
that's onomatopoeic.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.130
The bird actually does
have a vocalization,

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.750
that sounds like this.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.340
And the tail is entirely unique.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:53.710
There is no trogon species

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.800
with this sort of nicely curved tail.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.440
And the related species of trogon

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.930
is found on the Island of Hispaniola.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.750
So here is the Hispaniolan trogon.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.540
This one was chosen as a national bird

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.180
by the country of Haiti

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.870
and there they call it
Calecon Rouge or Calcon Rouge.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.070
And uh

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.874
here this is a male

00:01:17.874 --> 00:01:21.760
and same as with the Cuban trogon species

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.280
that is found in forested,

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.280
but more open habitats,
a nest in three cavities,

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.490
sometimes abandoned with pecker
holes and relatively common

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:39.490
both species are still in in
good shape, not endangered.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.010
On the same Island of Hispaniola,

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:44.490
the country of the Dominican Republic,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.120
Republica Dominicana chose the palmchat

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.200
to be their national bird.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.373
And the reason for this is

00:01:50.373 --> 00:01:52.160
that this is really part of the landscape.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.790
One of the most common birds

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:57.160
I recently visited the country
of the Dominican Republic

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.780
and got to see them
everywhere and their nests.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.020
Their massive fortresses of sticks

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.030
that are communal nests
found throughout the country

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.910
on telephone poles and in
trees, but mostly in palms

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.990
sometimes even on the rocks.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:16.410
I have seen this and this bird is a unique

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.250
kind of enigmatic fellow
genetically it's closest related

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.500
to North American waxwings

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.750
but it's a single member of it's family.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.990
So it's a good choice for a national bird.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:32.030
Then if we move to the
next island to the East,

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.941
to Puerto Rico.

00:02:33.941 --> 00:02:38.941
This bird the spindalis, was
chosen as a national bird.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:44.290
Let me see it's called
reinita mora in Puerto Rico

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.870
or reina mora, or sigua
another name I found.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:51.420
And in English, it used to be
called stripe-headed tanager.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.410
For obvious reason, it
has a lot of stripes,

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.770
but now we know that it's
not really closely related

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:57.680
to tanagers.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.950
It's a unique Caribbean lineage

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:02.700
and there are four species
on different islands.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.100
And this one is endemic to Puerto Rico.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.840
Then moving over to Jamaica.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.140
In Jamaica this gorgeous hummingbird

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.180
was chosen as a national bird.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:18.922
It's called by Jamaicans,
Dr. Bird or Dr. Hummingbird.

00:03:18.922 --> 00:03:21.572
I couldn't find a good
explanation for this name, but

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.380
the male has spectacularly long tail

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.330
and in flight must be a pleasure to see

00:03:30.528 --> 00:03:32.000
and be strangely enough,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.340
birds in the Eastern part of
the country have black bills

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.090
and the rest of the country, red bills.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.700
This one here is a bit faded

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.830
but it used to be a red billed bird.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.990
And female is without
this ornamental feathers

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:46.300
in the tail and a little bit smaller

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.550
but it's still a common
bird, not endangered.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:55.170
Then, let's maybe look at, oh
yeah, maybe here the parrots.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.220
So three different Caribbean countries

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.920
insular countries chose parrots
for their national birds.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.510
Let's maybe look at this one first.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.230
This is the largest of the
member of the genus amazona

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.360
imperial parrot, appropriately named.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.360
And this great bird is endemic
to the Island of Dominica.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.143
And there it's called
it's called sisserou.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.670
It's an endangered species.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.290
There are only fewer than 200
individuals left in the wild

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:28.720
and not too many in captivity.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.550
So this one has to be watched

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.870
and cared for managed carefully.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.050
The neighboring Island of
Saint Lucia chose their own

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:43.050
amazon totally differently
colored but still quite colorful.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.530
And also it's not very common.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.010
Let's see did the name they chose for it

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.220
the local name is jacquot or jacquot,

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:54.703
um I think it is jacquot.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:59.350
And in this case there
are fewer than 500 birds

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:00.550
left in the wild.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:03.970
And both of these birds
are in the mountains,

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.560
in the interior of the Island.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.820
So hopefully inaccessible

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.360
and hopefully the habit will be preserved.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.760
And then the third of these amazons

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:14.700
of the Caribbean,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.898
this parrot here called
Saint Vincent parrot

00:05:16.898 --> 00:05:20.840
on of the Island of Saint
Vincent and the Grenadines

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.080
has two different color
morphs, green and yellow.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.570
And here we have a
representative of the green

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.280
and these birds are a
little bit more common

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.440
not as endangered as the other two,

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:35.260
I mentioned before

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.020
but still it's a relatively
small populations.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.160
Population is small Island

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:42.213
and a little lower in this
case, not in the mountains.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.000
And then,

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.390
we go to

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:49.930
Island of Aruba

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:53.330
where interestingly two birds were chosen

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.163
for the national bird.

00:05:54.163 --> 00:05:58.080
First, it was the shoco,
locally named burrowing owl

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.340
subspecies endemic to Aruba.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.173
And

00:06:02.124 --> 00:06:06.070
the second one more recently,
this little parakeet,

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:10.923
brown throated parakeet which
there is called prikichi.

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.730
Probably onomatopoeic
probably after the call.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.270
And I guess the reason for the
switch was to bring attention

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.750
to this bird that maybe is
in more need of conservation.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:25.603
And that's why the country
brings attention to it.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.200
And finally, no not finally,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.910
but we are traveling further south.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:35.340
This is now Trinidad and two birds

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.390
here because Trinidad Island
has the scarlet ibis chosen

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:41.890
as their national bird.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:45.190
And the Island of Tobago

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.860
has this chachalaca.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.890
This is one of the guans,
chicken like birds.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:55.363
And this one is called rufous tail tailed,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.480
rufus caudatus in Latin,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.980
because of this nice rufous tipped tail.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:03.003
And also the belly is chestnut colored.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:07.093
This specimen is maybe not the nicest.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.600
The ibis is a common
site throughout Trinidad,

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.760
and it's a marsh bird
nesting in big colonies

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.790
and often seen in flocks,

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:21.930
traveling from the feeding
grounds to the, to their nests.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:25.800
And I then will move
to these two big birds.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.800
So the Island of Barbados
and the country of Barbados,

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:32.680
let me see here

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:35.740
chose the brown pelican
as their a national bird.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.100
So here, maybe actually this view

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.610
this is a species that is
pretty widely distributed

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.380
not only in Caribbean islands

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:46.060
but also in South Southern United States.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:49.250
And it's a colonial nester that I mean,

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:50.950
everybody talks about egg thinning

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.660
and endangerment of raptors,

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:55.630
but this bird also suffered quite a bit

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:58.800
in 1950s, 1970s from the use of DDT

00:07:58.800 --> 00:07:59.970
and

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:00.803
from

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.670
the pesticides and egg thinning.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.120
And finally,

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.573
here is the bird,

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:12.120
chosen by the country of uh

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.950
Antigua and Barbuda.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.810
And this is the magnificent

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:20.040
magnificent frigatebird
sometimes called man of war.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:24.640
This is one of the seabirds
famous for its behavior

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:27.170
of stealing food from other birds.

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:30.700
It can steal fish or some other,

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.210
but they can also catch their own fish.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:35.810
They can pick up for
example, snap, a flying fish

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:36.800
from the air.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.700
And this bird here is a male.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.370
In life, this would be a
bright red inflatable sack.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:44.320
And the males on the breeding grounds,

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.190
would inflate this to attract females.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.110
Females don't have this inflatable sack.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:54.490
And I will end by saying that
a one cool kind of record

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:58.610
that this bird also beats is
that a frigatebird's skeleton

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:00.470
is lighter than the feathers.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:02.800
If you actually plucked all
the feathers and combined them,

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.854
the skeleton is so primatized
and so light fused,

00:09:05.854 --> 00:09:07.390
that it's lighter.

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:11.221
So that's a pretty good choice
also for the national bird.

00:09:11.221 --> 00:09:13.804
(upbeat music)

